+# Build loading and unloading
+
+## Performance report: server freeze when a player with a big build joins or leaves
+
+**Workload**: one player joining with the 20,000-block test build from
+`SavedBuilds.luau`, on an 8-player server.
+
+**Bottleneck**: Script time in `Builds.server.luau`. The server MicroProfiler capture showed
+one 2,100 ms frame, almost all of it under `Script_Builds`. The old `loadBuild` created the
+whole save in one frame, and each block was 3 instances (a `Part`, an `Owner` `StringValue`,
+and a `PlacedAt` `NumberValue`). That is 60,000 instances. Each `Part` was parented by
+`Instance.new("Part", parent)` before its properties were set, so every property write also
+went through replication, physics, and rendering. Clients then got all 60,000 instances in
+one burst, which caused the `Replicator ProcessPackets` hitch. Leaving read `block.Position`
+from 20,000 parts and destroyed the whole folder in one frame.
+
+| Metric | Before | After |
+|---|---|---|
+| Server frame on join, 20k build | 2,100 ms (your capture) | not measured yet, see below |
+| Instances per block | 3 | 1 |
+| Instances replicated per 20k build | 60,000 | 20,000 |
+| Most blocks created or destroyed in one frame | 20,000 | 600 (and no more than about 4 ms of server time) |
+| Server memory for the value objects, 8 full builds | about 190 MB (2 × 20,000 × 8 × ~600 B) | 0 (a Luau table entry per block instead) |
+
+The "After" numbers come from reading the code, not from a profiler. I could not run Studio
+from this session, so the frame times still need a capture (see "For you to verify").
+
+## Changes
+
+Highest level first:
+
+1. **Removed the per-block `Owner` and `PlacedAt` value objects.** The server keeps owner ID,
+   placed time, cell, and plot in a Luau table (`blockInfos`), keyed by the block's part.
+   This removes two thirds of the instances the server creates and clients have to receive.
+   The owner check on `RemoveBlock` now reads this table. A client cannot write to it, so the
+   check is at least as strict as before. `PlacedAt` is kept for every block and loaded
+   from the save as before.
+2. **Load and unload a few hundred blocks per frame.** A single queue handles every plot
+   that is loading or unloading. Each frame it does up to 600 blocks or about 4 ms of server
+   time, whichever comes first, and continues on the next frame. Unloads run before loads,
+   and each kind runs in the order it arrived. A 20,000-block build takes roughly 35 to 60
+   frames, which is about 0.6 to 1 s. The 600-block cap also limits how much each client
+   has to process in one frame. If a step throws (for example, a malformed entry once saves
+   come from the DataStore), the queue logs a warning and drops that job. A failed unload
+   still frees its plot slot and cells, so one bad job can't stall every later load and
+   unload.
+3. **Set properties before `Parent`** when creating a block.
+4. **Unload no longer reads from the parts.** The cells to free come from the table, not
+   from `block.Position`.
+
+Placing and removing blocks work as before: same validation, same reach and rate limits,
+same owner rule.
+
+## What players will notice
+
+- **A big build appears over about a second** after its owner joins, instead of all at once
+  after a server-wide freeze. While it fills in, the build is partly there.
+- **A departed player's build disappears over about a second** instead of all at once.
+  Blocks in it can't be removed once the owner has left. Their cells stay blocked until each
+  block is gone, so nobody can place a block inside one that is still visible.
+- **If someone places a block in a plot while its build is still loading**, the placed block
+  keeps its cell and the saved block for that cell is skipped. The old code could not hit
+  this case because loading finished in one frame.
+- **On a full server**, a player who joins right as another leaves waits for the leaver's
+  plot to finish clearing (about a second) before their own plot is assigned. Unloads run
+  before queued loads, so this wait stays short even while other builds are loading. The
+  player keeps waiting for as long as they're in the game. After 15 s the server logs a
+  "no free plot yet" warning.
+- **Clients no longer see `Owner` and `PlacedAt` children under blocks.** No client code read
+  them. If the upcoming feature needs the placed time on clients, set it as an attribute on
+  the part before parenting it (about 24 bytes per block when it replicates), or send it
+  when a player asks for it. Don't bring back the value objects.
+
+## Left alone
+
+- `SavedBuilds.load` builds 20,000 tables in the test stub. That is a few milliseconds and
+  goes away when it's replaced by the real DataStore load. When that load ships, it needs a
+  `pcall` with retries. It also needs a flag that blocks saving a plot whose load failed,
+  or an empty plot could overwrite a player's real build.
+- One `Part` per block stays. Blocks have to be removable one at a time, so merging them
+  would change how the game plays.
+- Clients still receive all 20,000 parts per build, about 160,000 for 8 full builds. If join
+  time or client memory becomes the problem next, the bigger change is instance streaming
+  or having the server send block data and clients build the parts. Both are design
+  changes, so they were not made here.
+
+## For you to verify
+
+- Take a server MicroProfiler capture with the same workload: one player joins with the
+  20k build, then leaves. Check the worst frame under `Script_Builds`. The target is under
+  about 10 ms. If frames are still long, lower `QUEUE_ALLOWANCE` or `MAX_BLOCKS_PER_FRAME`.
+- Take a client capture on a phone with a published build, and check `ProcessPackets`
+  while someone's build loads. If it still hitches, lower `MAX_BLOCKS_PER_FRAME`.
+- Check how long a full build takes to appear and whether that's acceptable. If it loads
+  too slowly, raise the two constants.
+- Test 8 players joining at once. Builds load one after another, so the eighth build
+  appears after roughly 8 s.
